From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tamnd <tamnd@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 22:44:42 +0800
Subject: bazel: constrain the cc toolchains to the OS they can actually target

None of the three registered `toolchain()` rules had a `target_compatible_with`,
so as far as toolchain resolution is concerned each of them is willing to build
for any target platform at all. That has never mattered, because the only target
platforms in the tree are the ones the toolchains are for.

It starts mattering the moment a fourth OS exists. A build with a Windows target
platform on a Linux host resolves `linux-x86_64-toolchain`, because its execution
constraints match and it makes no claim about targets, and then compiles happily
with a Linux sysroot and Linux link flags. The result is ELF objects from a build
that was asked for PE, with no error anywhere. A missing toolchain is a two line
error message that names the platform. A wrong toolchain is a day.

Constrain by OS only. The two Linux entries already cross compile between Linux
CPUs, using the execution platform's clang against the target platform's sysroot,
which is what the `//:linux_aarch64` and `//:linux_x86_64` arms of the sysroot
`select()` are doing, so adding a CPU constraint here would take away something
that works today.

Nothing changes for any build that exists now. Both Linux entries still match a
Linux target and resolution still picks between them on the execution platform,
exactly as before.

Signed-off-by: tamnd <1218621+tamnd@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 bazel/internal/cc-toolchain/BUILD.bazel | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bazel/internal/cc-toolchain/BUILD.bazel b/bazel/internal/cc-toolchain/BUILD.bazel
index a91387bbbd..e3d2d3d2f6 100644
--- a/bazel/internal/cc-toolchain/BUILD.bazel
+++ b/bazel/internal/cc-toolchain/BUILD.bazel
@@ -164,6 +164,10 @@ toolchain(
         "@platforms//os:linux",
     ],
     tags = [TOP_LEVEL_TAG],  # Used from MODULE.bazel
+    # Constrained by OS and not by CPU. These toolchains already cross compile
+    # between Linux CPUs, using the execution platform's clang against the target
+    # platform's sysroot, and constraining the CPU here would take that away.
+    target_compatible_with = ["@platforms//os:linux"],
     toolchain = ":linux-aarch64_clang_toolchain",
     toolchain_type = "@bazel_tools//tools/cpp:toolchain_type",
 )
@@ -175,6 +179,7 @@ toolchain(
         "@platforms//os:linux",
     ],
     tags = [TOP_LEVEL_TAG],  # Used from MODULE.bazel
+    target_compatible_with = ["@platforms//os:linux"],
     toolchain = ":linux-x86_64_clang_toolchain",
     toolchain_type = "@bazel_tools//tools/cpp:toolchain_type",
 )
@@ -186,6 +191,7 @@ toolchain(
         "@platforms//os:macos",
     ],
     tags = [TOP_LEVEL_TAG],  # Used from MODULE.bazel
+    target_compatible_with = ["@platforms//os:macos"],
     toolchain = ":macos_clang_toolchain",
     toolchain_type = "@bazel_tools//tools/cpp:toolchain_type",
 )
